@* A demo session looks exactly like a signed-in one everywhere else, so this strip is the one
   honest tell — whose data this is, where changes go, and the ways out of it. Rendered by both
   shells: a demo visitor can wander onto the public pages too, and losing the ribbon there would
   leave them signed into sample data with nothing saying so.

   Renders nothing at all for anyone who is not in the demo, so both shells can include it
   unconditionally. *@

<AuthorizeView>
    <Authorized>
        @if (DemoClaims.IsDemo(context.User))
        {
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 border-b border-ember-300
                        bg-ember-100 px-4 py-2 text-sm text-ember-700">
                <span>
                    <span class="font-semibold">Demo island</span>
                    @* The sentence is the point of the strip, but on a phone it wraps to three
                       lines and eats the top of a 100vh shell — and the label plus the actions
                       already say what this is. *@
                    <span class="hidden sm:inline">
                        — sample data. Change anything; it stays in your private sandbox.
                    </span>
                </span>
                <span class="flex items-center gap-4">
                    @* The point of the whole feature: the one place a convinced visitor can act
                       on it. Signing in replaces the demo cookie, so the sandbox is left behind
                       to idle out on its own. *@
                    <a href="/login" class="btn btn-sm btn-primary">Create your account</a>
                    <a href="/demo/reset" class="font-medium underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-ember-600">Reset data</a>
                    <a href="/demo/exit" class="font-medium underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-ember-600">Exit demo</a>
                </span>
            </div>
        }
    </Authorized>
</AuthorizeView>
